IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `whs-worker`

-- Work Health and Safety Regulations 2011 (Commonwealth) -- the matters the Act leaves to the regulations, read from the
-- deposited Regulations instead of asked as inputs. A module beside whs-worker.l4; it calls that module's rules and restates
-- none of them.
--
-- SEAT: the worker, as in whs-worker.l4. "Is my illness one that must be notified? What happens under the default procedure
-- when I raise a safety issue? If I stopped unsafe work, which of my entitlements keep their continuity? Does the Act still
-- apply to my work for a former Comcare licensee?"
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 28, compilation date 25 March 2025 (register id F2025C00381), deposited at
-- ../../../work-health-and-safety-regulations-2011/registers/source-bundle/F2011L02664.txt (retrieved 2026-10-19).
--
-- IN: reg 12A (the end of the transitional period, s 12(6)(b)); regs 22, 23 (the agreed and default issue resolution
-- procedures, s 81(2)); reg 24 (the prescribed purposes, s 88); reg 699 (prescribed serious illnesses, s 36).
-- The Regulations prescribe no injury or illness out of s 36, no dangerous incident into or out of s 37, and no class of
-- worker under s 7(1)(i) (NOTES.md finding 14). Everything else in the Regulations (the hazard Chapters, licensing,
-- penalties) is the business's duty and out of this seat.

§ `Work Health and Safety Regulations 2011 -- the worker's questions`

§§ `reg 699, ss 35 to 38 -- is my illness a notifiable incident`

-- reg 699: "For section 36 of the Act, each of the following conditions is a serious illness: (a) any infection to which
-- the carrying out of work is a significant contributing factor, including any infection that is reliably attributable to
-- carrying out work: (i) that involves providing treatment or care to a person; or (ii) that involves contact with human
-- blood or body substances; or (iii) that involves handling or contact with animals, animal hides, skins, wool or hair,
-- animal carcasses or animal waste products; (b) the following occupational zoonoses contracted in the course of work
-- involving handling or contact with animals, ...: (i) Q fever; (ii) Anthrax; (iii) Leptospirosis; (iv) Brucellosis;
-- (v) Hendra Virus; (vi) Avian Influenza; (vii) Psittacosis." s 36 counts a prescribed illness as a "serious injury or
-- illness" whatever treatment it needed.
DECLARE `My illness` IS ONE OF
    `an infection to which my work was a significant contributing factor`
    `Q fever, anthrax, leptospirosis, brucellosis, Hendra virus, avian influenza or psittacosis, caught in work with animals or animal products`
    `another illness, or none`

GIVEN `illness` IS A `My illness`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `it is a prescribed serious illness` IF
    (NOT (`illness` EQUALS `another illness, or none`))

@export Is what happened at work a "notifiable incident" once the Work Health and Safety Regulations are applied (ss 35 to 38, reg 699)?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `What happened` @desc The most serious thing that happened: a death, an injury or illness needing the treatment listed, a dangerous incident listed in s 37, or something else
      `illness` IS A `My illness` @desc Whether anyone caught an infection to which the work was a significant contributing factor (for example in caring for a person, handling blood or body substances, or working with animals), or one of the occupational zoonoses the Regulations list
      `it arose out of the work of the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it arose out of the conduct of the business or undertaking
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it a notifiable incident under the Regulations` IF
    `Is it a notifiable incident` `what happened` `the regulations add it` `it arose out of the work of the business`
    WHERE
        -- whs-worker.l4's input reverses the Act's answer. The Regulations exclude nothing, so it is set only to add a
        -- prescribed illness that the Act's own list does not already catch.
        `the regulations add it` MEANS
            (NOT (`it is a notifiable incident` `what happened`)) AND `it is a prescribed serious illness` `illness`

§§ `regs 22, 23, ss 80 to 82 -- resolving a safety issue`

-- reg 22(2): "The agreed procedure for issue resolution at a workplace must include the steps set out in regulation 23."
-- (3): the business "must ensure that the agreed procedure ... (a) complies with subregulation (2); and (b) is set out in
-- writing; and (c) is communicated to all workers to whom the agreed procedure applies." Penalty: the tier G monetary penalty.
-- reg 23(2): "Any party to the issue may commence the procedure by telling each other party: (a) that there is an issue to be
-- resolved; and (b) the nature and scope of the issue." (3): "As soon as parties are told of the issue, all parties must meet or
-- communicate with each other to attempt to resolve the issue." (4): they must have regard to the degree and immediacy of
-- risk, the number and location of those affected, the temporary and permanent measures, and who will implement them. (5): a
-- party may be assisted or represented by a person it nominates. (6): "If the issue is resolved, details of the issue and its
-- resolution must be set out in a written agreement if any party to the issue requests this." (8): "A copy of the written
-- agreement must be given to: (a) all parties to the issue; and (b) if requested, to the health and safety committee". (9):
-- nothing prevents a worker bringing the issue to the worker's health and safety representative.
DECLARE `Issue party` IS ONE OF
    `I, the worker (or my representative)`
    `the business or undertaking`
    `my health and safety representative`

DECLARE `Issue step` IS ONE OF
    `tell each other party there is an issue, and its nature and scope`
    `meet or communicate to attempt to resolve the issue`
    `set out the issue and its resolution in a written agreement`
    `give each party a copy of the written agreement`
    `set out the agreed procedure in writing and tell the workers`

-- The default procedure, from the worker's side: I may start it (reg 23(2)); then the business must meet or communicate with
-- me (reg 23(3)). The Regulations give "as soon as" rather than a number of days, so no WITHIN is written.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Issue party` `Issue step`
`the default procedure, started by me` MEANS
    PARTY `I, the worker (or my representative)`
    MAY   `tell each other party there is an issue, and its nature and scope`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the business or undertaking`
        MUST  `meet or communicate to attempt to resolve the issue`
        LEST  BREACH BY `the business or undertaking` BECAUSE "reg 23(3): as soon as the parties are told of the issue, all parties must meet or communicate to attempt to resolve it"

-- reg 23(6), (8): once the issue is resolved and a party asks, the agreement must be written and a copy given to each party.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Issue party` `Issue step`
`the written agreement, when I ask for it` MEANS
    PARTY `the business or undertaking`
    MUST  `set out the issue and its resolution in a written agreement`
    HENCE
        PARTY `the business or undertaking`
        MUST  `give each party a copy of the written agreement`
        LEST  BREACH BY `the business or undertaking` BECAUSE "reg 23(8): a copy of the written agreement must be given to all parties to the issue"
    LEST  BREACH BY `the business or undertaking` BECAUSE "reg 23(6): details of the issue and its resolution must be set out in a written agreement if any party requests"

-- reg 22(3): the business's standing duty about an agreed procedure.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Issue party` `Issue step`
`the agreed procedure duty` MEANS
    PARTY `the business or undertaking`
    MUST  `set out the agreed procedure in writing and tell the workers`
    LEST  BREACH BY `the business or undertaking` BECAUSE "reg 22(3): the agreed procedure must include the reg 23 steps, be set out in writing and be communicated to all workers it applies to"

DECLARE `The default procedure for my issue` IS ONE OF
    `I may start it by telling the business (and any other party) that there is an issue and its nature and scope; all parties must then meet or communicate to try to resolve it, having regard to the risk, who is affected, the measures needed and who will carry them out; I may be assisted or represented by a person I nominate (reg 23(2)-(5))`
    `the agreed procedure applies, and it must include the default procedure's steps, be in writing and have been communicated to me (reg 22)`

@export I have a health and safety issue at work. What procedure must be followed to resolve it (s 81(2), regs 22, 23)?
GIVEN `there is an agreed procedure at my workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business has an agreed procedure for resolving issues at your workplace
GIVETH A `The default procedure for my issue`
`What procedure resolves my safety issue` MEANS
    IF `there is an agreed procedure at my workplace`
    THEN `the agreed procedure applies, and it must include the default procedure's steps, be in writing and have been communicated to me (reg 22)`
    ELSE `I may start it by telling the business (and any other party) that there is an issue and its nature and scope; all parties must then meet or communicate to try to resolve it, having regard to the risk, who is affected, the measures needed and who will carry them out; I may be assisted or represented by a person I nominate (reg 23(2)-(5))`

@export Once my safety issue is resolved, must the resolution be put in writing and given to me (reg 23(6), (8))?
GIVEN `the issue is resolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the issue has been resolved
      `a party asked for a written agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you, or any other party to the issue, asked for the details of the issue and its resolution to be set out in a written agreement
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the resolution be written and given to me` IF
    `the issue is resolved` AND `a party asked for a written agreement`

§§ `reg 24, s 88 -- my continuity of engagement after stopping unsafe work`

-- reg 24: "For section 88 of the Act, the prescribed purposes are the assessment of eligibility for, or the calculation of
-- benefits for, any benefit or entitlement associated with the worker's engagement, including: (a) remuneration and
-- promotion, as affected by seniority; (b) superannuation benefits; (c) leave entitlements; (d) any entitlement to notice of
-- termination of the engagement."
DECLARE `My entitlement` IS ONE OF
    `my pay or promotion, as it depends on seniority`
    `my superannuation benefits`
    `my leave entitlements`
    `my entitlement to notice of termination`
    `another benefit or entitlement associated with my engagement`

DECLARE `Continuity for my entitlement` IS ONE OF
    `protected: ceasing the unsafe work does not break my continuity of engagement for working out this entitlement (s 88, reg 24)`
    `not protected: I unreasonably failed to do safe and appropriate alternative work I was directed to do (s 88)`

@export I stopped unsafe work. Does that break my continuity of engagement for this entitlement (s 88, reg 24)?
GIVEN `the entitlement` IS A `My entitlement` @desc Which entitlement you are asking about: seniority-based pay or promotion, superannuation, leave, notice of termination, or another benefit associated with your engagement
      `I was directed to safe and appropriate alternative work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business directed you to suitable alternative work, at the same or another workplace, that was safe and appropriate for you
      `I unreasonably failed to do it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you unreasonably failed to comply with that direction (ignored if there was none)
GIVETH A `Continuity for my entitlement`
`Is my continuity kept for this entitlement` MEANS
    -- reg 24 reaches "any benefit or entitlement associated with the worker's engagement", so every value of `the entitlement`
    -- is a prescribed purpose; it is asked so the answer can name it.
    IF `Is my continuity of engagement protected` `I was directed to safe and appropriate alternative work` `I unreasonably failed to do it`
    THEN `protected: ceasing the unsafe work does not break my continuity of engagement for working out this entitlement (s 88, reg 24)`
    ELSE `not protected: I unreasonably failed to do safe and appropriate alternative work I was directed to do (s 88)`

§§ `reg 12A, s 12(6) -- does the Act still apply to my work for a former Comcare licensee`

-- reg 12A: "For the purposes of subsection 12(6) of the Act, the transitional period for the following non-Commonwealth
-- licensees ends on the day on which Part 2 of Schedule 1 to the Work Health and Safety Amendment (Managing Psychosocial Risk
-- and Other Measures) Regulations 2022 commences: (a) Hollard Insurance Partners Limited; (b) MLC Wealth Limited; (c) Vicinity
-- Centres PM Pty Ltd." The compilation's endnotes record that Schedule 1 of those Regulations commenced in two steps, items 1
-- and 2 on 1 April 2023 and the remainder on 4 January 2023, without saying which items are Part 2. So the period ended on 4
-- January 2023 or 1 April 2023: before 4 January 2023 it was running, from 1 April 2023 it has ended, and between the two the
-- deposit does not say (NOTES.md finding 15). For any other licensee the Regulations in force prescribe no day, so on the text
-- its period has not ended (s 12(6)(b)).
DECLARE `My licensee` IS ONE OF
    `Hollard Insurance Partners Limited`
    `MLC Wealth Limited`
    `Vicinity Centres PM Pty Ltd`
    `another non-Commonwealth licensee`

DECLARE `My licensee's transitional period` IS ONE OF
    `running: the Act applies to my work for it (s 12(4), (6))`
    `ended: the day reg 12A prescribes has passed, so the Act no longer applies to my work for it by s 12(4)`
    `not known from the deposit: the period ended on 4 January 2023 or 1 April 2023, and the Regulations' endnotes do not say which`

@export Is my employer's transitional period as a former Comcare licensee still running on a given day (s 12(6), reg 12A)?
GIVEN `the licensee` IS A `My licensee` @desc Which non-Commonwealth licensee you work for
      `the day` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about
GIVETH A `My licensee's transitional period`
`Is my licensee's transitional period running` MEANS
    IF `the licensee` EQUALS `another non-Commonwealth licensee`
    THEN `running: the Act applies to my work for it (s 12(4), (6))`
    ELSE IF `the day` LESS THAN (Date 4 1 2023)
         THEN `running: the Act applies to my work for it (s 12(4), (6))`
         ELSE IF `the day` AT LEAST (Date 1 4 2023)
              THEN `ended: the day reg 12A prescribes has passed, so the Act no longer applies to my work for it by s 12(4)`
              ELSE `not known from the deposit: the period ended on 4 January 2023 or 1 April 2023, and the Regulations' endnotes do not say which`
